This is a known Helm bug, not a problem with your chart — you're on the last version that has it. Here's what's going on, verified against the Helm source:

## Root cause

In Helm v3.1.2, `helm lint` renders your chart using the template engine **without a Kubernetes client** (see `pkg/lint/rules/template.go`, which calls plain `e.Render(...)`). The `lookup` function was only registered in the template function map when a cluster client was available:

```go
// pkg/engine/engine.go in v3.1.2
if e.config != nil {
    funcMap["lookup"] = NewLookupFunction(e.config)
}
```

Since lint has no cluster connection, `e.config` is nil, so `lookup` was never added to the func map at all. Go templates resolve functions at **parse** time, so any reference to `lookup` (even inside an `if` in `_helpers.tpl`) fails with `function "lookup" not defined` before your template logic ever runs. That's why `helm install`/`helm template` (which have a cluster client) work fine but `helm lint` doesn't.

## The fix

This was reported as helm/helm#7955 and fixed in PR #7969, released in **Helm v3.1.3** (the v3.1.3 release commit is literally titled "fix linting error with lookup function (#7969)"). The fix does two things:

1. Adds a **placeholder** `lookup` to the base func map (`pkg/engine/funcs.go`) that returns an empty map, so parsing succeeds without a cluster:
   ```go
   // Provide a placeholder for the "lookup" function, which requires a kubernetes
   // connection.
   "lookup": func(string, string, string, string) (map[string]interface{}, error) {
       return map[string]interface{}{}, nil
   },
   ```
2. Only registers the real, cluster-backed lookup when not linting and a client exists:
   ```go
   if !e.LintMode && e.config != nil {
       funcMap["lookup"] = NewLookupFunction(e.config)
   }
   ```

## What to do

**Upgrade Helm to v3.1.3 or later** (any modern 3.x release works — I verified the placeholder is present from v3.1.3 through current main). No chart changes needed.

One behavioral note after upgrading: during `helm lint` and `helm template` (without `--validate`), `lookup` will always return an empty map (`{}`), so any conditional like `{{- if (lookup "v1" "ConfigMap" .Release.Namespace "foo") }}` will take the "not found" branch. That's by design — lint has no cluster to query — and your chart logic will still work correctly at actual install/upgrade time when the real lookup is in play.

